---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Pipeline from '../components/Pipeline.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { thesis, hypernetworks } from '../data/research';
import { url } from '../lib/urls';
import { getPosts } from '../lib/posts';
const thesisArticle = (await getPosts()).find(
  (post) => post.id === 'abduction-and-the-search-space',
);
---

<Base
  title="Research"
  description="Research on neuro-symbolic abductive inference for moral reasoning and input encodings for hypernetworks by Fikri Budianto."
>
  <header class="page-heading">
    <h1>Research</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      My research interests include deep learning, natural language processing, knowledge
      representation and reasoning, and neuro-symbolic AI. The following work examines neural
      reasoning systems and input encodings for hypernetworks.
    </p>
  </header>
  <article class="research-block" id="thesis">
    <p class="project-category">
      {thesis.type} · {thesis.institution}
    </p>
    <h2>{thesis.title}</h2>
    <p class="lead">{thesis.description}</p>
    <div class="research-body">
      <section>
        <h3>The problem</h3>
        <p>
          Language models can produce plausible reasoning without a formally verifiable inference
          process. A neuro-symbolic approach makes the premises and inference steps explicit,
          opening them to inspection.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>The approach</h3>
        <p>
          ARGOS iteratively asks an LLM for commonsense premises, translates them into logical
          clauses, and uses a SAT solver to check whether the accumulated knowledge establishes a
          target query.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <Pipeline />
    <div class="research-body">
      <section>
        <h3>My work</h3>
        <p>
          I adapted ARGOS to moral reasoning on ExplainEthics. Because the dataset has no explicit
          quantification, I changed the logical representation from first-order to propositional
          logic. Without entities to constrain candidate generation, commonsense clause generation
          instead considers combinations of literals.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Limitations</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Propositionalisation expands the abductive search space combinatorially.</li>
          <li>Generated premises can fail to move inference toward the target query.</li>
          <li>Stochastic commonsense generation can produce inconsistent clauses.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
    <h3 class="results-heading">Reported results</h3>
    <table class="results-table">
      <caption>Accuracy on{thesis.queries}evaluated ExplainEthics queries</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Method</th>
          <th scope="col">Accuracy</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">ARGOS</th>
          <td>{thesis.argosAccuracy}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Chain-of-Thought baseline</th>
          <td>{thesis.baselineAccuracy}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="source-note">
      Results from the reported thesis experiment on ExplainEthics. This evaluation describes the
      tested configuration and query set; broader generalization remains a separate question.
    </p>
    {thesisArticle && (
      <p style="margin-top:24px">
        <a class="text-link primary" href={url('writing/abduction-and-the-search-space/')}>
          Article: abductive reasoning and search-space growth <Icon name="arrow" />
        </a>
      </p>
    )}
  </article>
  <article class="research-block" id="hypernetworks">
    <p class="project-category">{hypernetworks.type} · IR–NLP Laboratory, UI</p>
    <h2>{hypernetworks.title}</h2>
    <p class="lead">{hypernetworks.description}</p>
    <div class="research-body">
      <section>
        <h3>Research question</h3>
        <p>
          How does the choice of input encoding affect the learning of continuous neural-network
          weight manifolds? The study compares positional encoding, Gaussian random Fourier
          features, and multiresolution hash encoding.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>The findings</h3>
        <p>
          In the manuscript’s experiments, Gaussian random Fourier features yielded the strongest
          validation accuracy, while hash encoding achieved lower loss metrics. The comparison
          highlights a tradeoff between reconstruction fidelity and downstream accuracy, alongside
          implementation overhead in PyTorch.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>My contribution</h3>
        <p>
          As an AI research intern, I reviewed the relevant literature, implemented encoding
          comparisons, ran controlled experiments, and communicated the findings through reports,
          presentations, and the research manuscript.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Scope</h3>
        <p>
          This is a research manuscript. Experiments were bounded by computational resources, and
          the manuscript identifies evaluation on new networks as future work.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
